course: dictionary
lesson: 13560
title: Inaccessible: hard to reach, hard to use
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Inaccessible means impossible or very hard to reach or get into. It can also mean hard to understand. Look at the word parts: «in-» means not, and «accessible» means able to be reached or used, so inaccessible means not able to be reached or used.
explain: «The mountain village was inaccessible after the heavy snow, because the road was blocked.» The clues are blocked and snow: nobody could get in, so inaccessible means impossible to reach.
explain: «The lift was broken, so the upstairs rooms were inaccessible to Sam in his wheelchair.» Here inaccessible describes a place that people cannot easily use. Good buildings are accessible, with ramps, lifts and wide doors, so everyone can get in.
explain: «The instructions were so full of long words that they were inaccessible to young readers.» Here the meaning is hard to understand. Use the clues around the word to check: long words and young readers tell you the text was too hard to follow.
fact: Inaccessible means impossible or very hard to reach or get into.
fact: Inaccessible can also mean hard to understand.
fact: A building, place or website that is inaccessible cannot be used easily by disabled people, for example with no ramp for wheelchairs.
fact: The opposite of inaccessible is accessible: a place that is accessible can be reached and used easily.
fact: The prefix «in-» means not, so you can break the word into parts to check its meaning.
exercise: «The mountain village was inaccessible after the heavy snow.» What does inaccessible mean here? | Full of friendly people | *Impossible to reach | Cheap to visit | Very bright and colourful
exercise: «The lift was broken, so the upstairs rooms were inaccessible to Sam in his wheelchair.» Why were the rooms inaccessible? | The rooms had been freshly painted | Sam did not want to go upstairs | The rooms were too small to see | *Sam could not get up to them easily
exercise: Which word is the opposite of inaccessible? | Ancient | Heavy | *Accessible | Noisy
test: «The instructions were so full of long words that they were inaccessible to young readers.» What does inaccessible mean here? | Written in pencil | *Hard to understand | Too short to finish | Easy to read aloud
test: A website that screen readers cannot use is what kind of website? | Free to use | *Inaccessible | Accessible | Brilliant
exam: A building with no ramp or lift is ___ to people in wheelchairs. Which word fits the gap? | Colourful | *Inaccessible | Old | Accessible
